1
00:00:00,124 --> 00:00:02,852
Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:02,852 --> 00:00:05,977
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,977 --> 00:00:10,376
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:10,938 --> 00:00:15,706
Four of the biggest technology companies in the world are spending hundreds of

5
00:00:15,706 --> 00:00:18,246
billions of dollars this year on one thing:

6
00:00:18,246 --> 00:00:20,784
computers for artificial intelligence.

7
00:00:20,784 --> 00:00:23,356
Will that money ever come back?

8
00:00:24,343 --> 00:00:25,857
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah.

9
00:00:25,857 --> 00:00:31,034
This is an A-I-generated version of my voice, and it is narrating today's story.

10
00:00:32,127 --> 00:00:34,542
Let's start with what is happening.

11
00:00:34,542 --> 00:00:40,136
Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon and Meta are building huge buildings full of computer

12
00:00:40,136 --> 00:00:41,523
chips, called data centres.

13
00:00:41,523 --> 00:00:47,810
Alphabet, the parent of Google, now says it will spend between one hundred and

14
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:52,202
ninety-five and two hundred and five billion dollars this year on this.

15
00:00:52,202 --> 00:00:55,070
Amazon says about two hundred and twenty billion.

16
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:59,987
Meta says between one hundred and thirty and one hundred and forty-five billion.

17
00:00:59,987 --> 00:01:04,218
Spending like this is called capital expenditure, or capex:

18
00:01:04,218 --> 00:01:09,333
money spent on things that last for years, like buildings and machines, rather than

19
00:01:09,333 --> 00:01:10,327
day to day costs.

20
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:11,715
Why so much?

21
00:01:11,715 --> 00:01:16,096
Think of these companies as landlords of computing power.

22
00:01:16,096 --> 00:01:19,182
Businesses that want to train or run A-I do not buy their own chips.

23
00:01:19,182 --> 00:01:24,822
They rent space on someone else's computers, through what is called the cloud.

24
00:01:24,822 --> 00:01:28,712
The more companies rent, the more the landlords must build.

25
00:01:28,712 --> 00:01:34,000
Amazon's boss has even pointed to higher memory chip costs as one reason its bill

26
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:34,600
went up.

27
00:01:35,119 --> 00:01:37,388
Now here is the part that matters:

28
00:01:37,388 --> 00:01:38,759
the rent is coming in.

29
00:01:38,759 --> 00:01:44,677
In the April to June quarter, Amazon's cloud business, A-W-S, grew thirty-seven per

30
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:48,631
cent from a year earlier, its fastest in about four and a half years.

31
00:01:48,631 --> 00:01:51,108
Google's cloud business grew eighty-two per cent.

32
00:01:51,108 --> 00:01:55,031
Microsoft's Azure cloud grew forty-three per cent in its latest quarter.

33
00:01:55,031 --> 00:01:56,801
So the spending is not random.

34
00:01:56,801 --> 00:02:01,889
The companies say customers are asking for more computing than they can supply.

35
00:02:02,528 --> 00:02:03,891
So why the worry?

36
00:02:03,891 --> 00:02:07,978
Because spending comes first and earnings come later.

37
00:02:07,978 --> 00:02:10,794
Imagine opening a restaurant chain.

38
00:02:10,794 --> 00:02:15,251
You build fifty kitchens before you know how many diners will turn up.

39
00:02:15,251 --> 00:02:16,728
If diners come, great.

40
00:02:16,728 --> 00:02:21,123
If they come more slowly than you planned, you are stuck with the cost of the

41
00:02:21,123 --> 00:02:21,782
kitchens.

42
00:02:21,782 --> 00:02:24,089
That is the fear in markets.

43
00:02:24,089 --> 00:02:28,394
Last week chip company shares fell sharply, and Meta's shares dropped on worries

44
00:02:28,394 --> 00:02:30,344
about its A-I spending.

45
00:02:30,928 --> 00:02:32,706
One more thing makes this tricky:

46
00:02:32,706 --> 00:02:34,827
chips do not last forever.

47
00:02:34,827 --> 00:02:39,390
A building can serve for decades, but the computers inside it get old and need

48
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:42,344
replacing within a few years.

49
00:02:42,344 --> 00:02:46,571
Companies spread that cost over those years, which means today's spending keeps

50
00:02:46,571 --> 00:02:49,251
showing up in their profits for a long time.

51
00:02:49,251 --> 00:02:51,732
So the bet is not only that demand grows.

52
00:02:51,732 --> 00:02:56,468
It is that demand grows fast enough to pay for machines that will need replacing

53
00:02:56,468 --> 00:02:57,370
before long.

54
00:02:57,971 --> 00:03:00,115
Here is what would count as paying back.

55
00:03:00,115 --> 00:03:02,577
First, cloud growth that keeps up, quarter after quarter.

56
00:03:02,577 --> 00:03:06,082
Second, customers paying for A-I tools, not just trying them.

57
00:03:06,082 --> 00:03:09,805
Third, the spending levelling off as demand catches up.

58
00:03:09,805 --> 00:03:11,876
Any one of these is a good sign.

59
00:03:11,876 --> 00:03:13,468
None of them is certain.

60
00:03:14,052 --> 00:03:16,731
There is also a limit to what we know.

61
00:03:16,731 --> 00:03:21,709
Most of these numbers are what the companies themselves have said, and plans can

62
00:03:21,709 --> 00:03:22,309
change.

63
00:03:22,279 --> 00:03:29,121
Whether A-I will earn enough to justify this spending is something nobody can say

64
00:03:29,121 --> 00:03:29,721
today.

65
00:03:29,691 --> 00:03:31,940
That is an open question, not a fact.

66
00:03:32,505 --> 00:03:37,657
Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon report their latest results later this week, and the

67
00:03:37,657 --> 00:03:41,805
cloud numbers and any change to their spending plans are the thing to watch.

68
00:03:42,483 --> 00:03:43,492
To sum up:

69
00:03:43,492 --> 00:03:47,680
the spending is real and the rent is arriving, but whether it pays back is still a

70
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:50,418
test, and this week's results are the next exam.

71
00:03:51,067 --> 00:03:55,326
This is news and explanation, not investment advice.

72
00:03:56,359 --> 00:03:59,424
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode.

